      "title": "Intrinsic Value and Margin of Safety",
      "description": "Bill Ackman argues that a security's value is the present value of the cash flows it generates over its lifetime, akin to a bond's predictable payout. Because future predictions are uncertain, investors must demand a margin of safety by purchasing businesses at a deep discount to their estimated value, ensuring they avoid permanent capital loss even if their projections are flawed."
    },
    {
      "title": "Moats and the Risk of Disruption",
      "description": "Bill Ackman argues that identifying wide moats is the hardest part of investing, especially in an era of rapid technological disruption. To survive, businesses must possess enduring consumer demand, high barriers to entry, and proven operating systems that enable scale without constant capital injections."
    },
